    <div class="faq-section">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">Does alcohol affect muscle growth after 40?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">Heavy drinking does. In the key study, binge-level drinking (roughly 9–12 drinks) right after a workout cut muscle protein synthesis by 24–37% over the next 8 hours. Moderate drinking looks far less costly — about 3 drinks after hard training didn't affect strength recovery in another study. After 40, when muscle already responds less to protein, it's worth keeping heavy nights away from training days.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">How many drinks affects muscle protein synthesis?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">The clearest damage was shown at binge levels, about 1.5g of alcohol per kg of bodyweight (roughly 9–12 drinks). Around 0.5g/kg (about 3 drinks) showed no effect on strength recovery, while about twice that made strength loss worse. Low doses haven't been studied much, but the cost clearly scales with the dose — and drinking after a workout is worse than drinking on a rest day.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">Does alcohol cancel out protein intake?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">No. Heavy drinking reduces how effectively your body uses the protein you eat, but protein still helps — in the research, the same heavy drinking cut muscle building by 37% with carbs and only 24% with protein. Hit your protein target on nights you drink, and eat some of it with the drinks.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">What is the best strategy for drinking and maintaining muscle after 40?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">Three strategies help most: (1) Don't drink heavily on the same day as a hard workout. (2) Eat protein before and with your drinks, not just after. (3) Keep it to 1–2 drinks near a training session. The goal isn't abstinence — it's knowing the cost and managing it.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">Is beer or wine worse for muscle protein synthesis?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">The effect is driven by the amount of alcohol, not the type of drink. A standard beer, a glass of wine, and a shot of spirits each contain roughly the same amount of alcohol. What differs is how quickly you drink it and how many you have.</div>
      </div>

